StringVar() is a special variable class that links a python variable to a Tkinter widget 
(like entry or label). It automatically updates the widget when the variable changes 
and vise versa. 
2. The grid() layout arranges widgets in table-like structures of rows and columns 
making it easier to align the buttons neatly. 
3. When a button is clicked it is associated with the function command. Clicking the 
button calls btn_click(#) which updates the stringVar() to reflect the number or 
operator. 
4. Errors such as division by zero or invalid expressions are handles using a try-except 
block in btn_equal(). A way to improve the code would be more descriptive 
messages like “cannot divide by zero” or prevent invalid input by restricting certain 
input by users that can result in an error.
5. Some improvements include aligning buttons uniformly with equal size and 
padding. You can also highlight the equals or clear button with a different color and 
add a backspace button to correct mistakes without clearing everything. 
6. Pack() arranges widgets in a single row or column which is not ideal for a multi-row 
layout. Place() uses positioning which makes it restrictive and harder to resize. Grid() 
is best for this application because it suits table-like layouts and aligns buttons 
consistently. It also adjusts the size automatically if the window is resized. 
7.
• btn_click(item)→ Adds the clicked button’s value to the current expression and 
updates the display.
• btn_clear()→ clears the current input and resets the display
• btn_equal()→ Evaluates the current expression, updates the display with the 
result or shows an error message if invalid. 
